On Fri, 30 Dec 2005, Bill Wayne wrote:
> Panu Lahtinen wrote:
[Re: Cursed-wand effects]
>> For attack wands (MM, striking, fire, cold) the direction could be
>> randomized in a similar way that for cursed projectile weapons.

   Evil!  Especially for the bouncy and area-effect spells that can kill
a low-level character or seriously inconvenience a medium-level character
if they hit. ("Oops, that wand of fire was cursed! You didn't really
want those scrolls, did you?")

>> This might be too evil, but wands that have a spell counterpart in matter
>> school, could zap you (with a certain, small, propability):

   Too evil... gee, you think? :)  My tamer suggestions follow.  I would
like to note that all these suggestions tend to make BUC-IDing of wands
easier, if that matters.
   A very easy cursed-wand effect would be to make cursed wands explode
sometimes (say, 20% of the time?), with all accompanying effects. Of
course, that wouldn't address the original purpose of this brainstorming
session, which was to make cursed /oLight cast darkness instead.


>> /oOpening: "You vomit!", lose some nutrition

   Cursed /oOpening has a 30-50% chance of destroying the door entirely.

>> /oLocking: "As you zap the wand, your eyes are covered with scales! You
>>             can't see!"

   Bizarrely evil. I have no suggestion.

>> /oDig: "Your body is covered in deep wounds", lose HPs (note the spell
>>         cast by some monsters)

   Cursed /oDig could dig a pit instead of a hole... unless of course
a majority of players would /prefer/ the pit, but I doubt that.


>> /oNothing: "Something seems to happen...", just a message

   "Something happens."  (In any case, shouldn't this be added as a YAFM
when hallucinating?  Alternatively: "A hollow voice whispers, "Fool.")

>> /oSDD: "Suddenly, all the doors vanish!", all closed, open and broken
>>        doors are changed to hidden doors.

   Besides the advantage that monsters won't open hidden doors, ISTR
that broken doors still count as doorways for the purposes of moving
diagonally, in which case I'd sometimes want to replace them with new
closed doors that I could force-bolt or kick down. ;)


>> /oTele: propability for a level teleport

   I like this one. Just note that it's the target that gets teleported,
not necessarily yourself. I don't know if the code currently supports
level-teleporting objects.


>> /oEnlightenment: possibility to lose a point of Int

   This one, I also don't think is too evil --- if it's Wis and not Int.
"You feel unenlightened."

> /oDeath: "A beam of energy comes out of the rear! You irridate yourself
> in pure energy! DYWYPI?"

   Evil.
   Cursed /oDeath could act as a wand of sleep instead.

> /oUndeadturning: "You suddenly turn into a zombie!" Slow movement,
> vulnerability to fire for a short period of time.

   Bizarre, and would require savefile tweaking.
   Could act as /oDeath, except obviously that's way too powerful.
Could summon a couple of undead.

> /oWishing: "<name> rods fall from the ceiling." All enemies get
> /oDeaths.

   Amusing, but obviously far too evil. :)  Now, if a monster actually
/had/ a wand of wishing, I'd fully expect it to wish for a wand of
death...
   There are enough ways wishing can go wrong that there's no need for
a special effect here --- just give the player a -3 GDSM, or a blessed
rustproof +5 pair of kicking boots, or whatever.

> /oCancellation: "The wand spins in your hand before firing!" Acts as if
> you used it on yourself. || "The wand seems plainer." Cancels itself.

   Inconsistent: /oCancellation, like ?o and +o, can't be cancelled.
I have no suggestion.

> /oCreateMonster: "You hear a far off hissing..." Creates a dozen
> monsters in a different room. Cockatrices would be pretty nasty for
> this one.

   ...unless the player has gloves. ;)

> /oProbing: "The wand glows for a second..." All monsters instantly know
> your location.

   Okay; I certainly think aggravation should be a side effect of zapping
a cursed /oInvis or /oTele. But consider that the only time a player
ever zaps a wand of probing is when he's next to a monster anyway. I'm
not sure how other people use /oProbing (if at all), but I don't think
this effect would be noticeable the way I play.

> /oTeleportation: "Creatures appear from nowhere!" Takes creatures from
> other parts of the level and transport them adjacent to you.

   Sacfest!

> /oMakeInvisible: "Everything else disappears!" Blindness.

   Nah. (YAFNI, though: If the player has telepathy and all invisible 
monsters on the level are thinking, then cursed !oSI may confer blindness
instead of see-invisible.)

> /oPolyMorph: I dunno. Perhaps it should prompt you for a monster, and
> change you to a random one anyway? That or polymorph everything nearby.

   Greater chance of polymorphing into (water|blank paper|ring of 
hunger|...).  Or: The polymorphed item always comes out cursed.

-Arthur


